How can we use the phrase "nothing can stop me" in a C++ program to handle exceptions and ensure that the program continues to run despite errors? Can you provide an example of how this phrase can be incorporated into a try-catch block or other error-handling mechanism in C++ code?
Assuming you want to ensure that your program continues to run even if an exception is thrown, you can use a try-catch block with the catch statement set to catch all exceptions. Here's an example:
try {
  // code that might throw an exception
} catch (...) {
  std::cerr << "nothing can stop me" << std::endl;
}
The ellipsis in the catch statement means that it will catch any exception, regardless of its type. This way, your program will keep running even if an unexpected error occurs.
Alternatively, you can define your own exception class that doesn't actually stop the program from running. Here's an example:
class NothingCanStopMeException : public std::exception {
public:
  const char* what() const throw() {
    return "nothing can stop me";
  }
};
Then you can throw this exception in your code, and catch it if necessary:
try {
  // code that might throw an exception
  throw NothingCanStopMeException();
} catch (const std::exception& ex) {
  std::cerr << ex.what() << std::endl;
}
This will print "nothing can stop me" to the console, but won't actually stop the program from running.

