One way to handle multiple types of items and their prices in Java is to create a class for each item type that includes the item's price and any other relevant information. Then, you can create an ArrayList or HashMap to store instances of each item type class along with their quantities. Here's an example code:
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
public class Item {
    private String name;
    private double price;
    public Item(String name, double price) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }
}
public class ItemSummary {
    private HashMap<Item, Integer> items;
    public ItemSummary() {
        items = new HashMap<Item, Integer>();
    }
    public void addItem(Item item, int quantity) {
        items.put(item, quantity);
    }
    public double getTotalCost() {
        double total = 0;
        for (Item item : items.keySet()) {
            total += item.getPrice() * items.get(item);
        }
        return total;
    }
    public void printSummary() {
        System.out.println("Item Type\t|\tQuantity\t|\tPrice\t|\tTotal Cost");
        System.out.println("--------------------------------------------------------");
        for (Item item : items.keySet()) {
            int quantity = items.get(item);
            double price = item.getPrice();
            double totalCost = quantity * price;
            System.out.println(item.getName() + "\t|\t" + quantity + "\t|\t$" + price + "\t|\t$" + totalCost);
        }
        System.out.println("--------------------------------------------------------");
        System.out.println("Total Cost:\t$" + getTotalCost());
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Item item1 = new Item("Item 1", 4.0);
        Item item2 = new Item("Item 2", 2.5);
        Item item3 = new Item("Item 3", 8.0);
        ItemSummary summary = new ItemSummary();
        summary.addItem(item1, 10);
        summary.addItem(item2, 5);
        summary.addItem(item3, 2);
        summary.printSummary();
    }
}
```
In this example, the `Item` class represents each item type with its name and price. The `ItemSummary` class stores a HashMap of items and their quantities, and includes methods for calculating the total cost and printing a summary. The `main` method creates instances of each item type and adds them to an `ItemSummary` object, then prints the summary. 
This code can be easily extended to handle a large number of item types and quantities by adding more instances of the `Item` class and calling the `addItem` method for each item with its corresponding quantity.

To generate 5-word lists for each of the 5 topics in a scalable and efficient manner, we can use a data structure called a hash set. A hash set is a data structure that stores unique values in an unordered manner. We can use a hash set to efficiently store unique words for each topic and then sort them alphabetically.
Here's an example implementation in Go:
```go
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "sort"
)
func main() {
    foods := []string{"pizza", "sushi", "hamburger", "taco", "ramen"}
    cities := []string{"New York", "Paris", "Tokyo", "London", "Beijing"}
    books := []string{"To Kill a Mockingbird", "Pride and Prejudice", "1984", "The Great Gatsby", "The Catcher in the Rye"}
    colors := []string{"blue", "green", "red", "yellow", "purple"}
    professions := []string{"doctor", "teacher", "engineer", "lawyer", "artist"}
    // Initialize empty hash sets to store unique words for each topic
    foodsSet := make(map[string]bool)
    citiesSet := make(map[string]bool)
    booksSet := make(map[string]bool)
    colorsSet := make(map[string]bool)
    professionsSet := make(map[string]bool)
    // Loop through each topic and add unique words to corresponding hash set
    for _, topic := range [][]string{foods, cities, books, colors, professions} {
        for _, word := range topic {
            switch topic {
            case foods:
                foodsSet[word] = true
            case cities:
                citiesSet[word] = true
            case books:
                booksSet[word] = true
            case colors:
                colorsSet[word] = true
            case professions:
                professionsSet[word] = true
            }
        }
    }
    // Convert hash sets to sorted arrays
    foodsList := make([]string, 0, len(foodsSet))
    for word := range foodsSet {
        foodsList = append(foodsList, word)
    }
    sort.Strings(foodsList)
    citiesList := make([]string, 0, len(citiesSet))
    for word := range citiesSet {
        citiesList = append(citiesList, word)
    }
    sort.Strings(citiesList)
    booksList := make([]string, 0, len(booksSet))
    for word := range booksSet {
        booksList = append(booksList, word)
    }
    sort.Strings(booksList)
    colorsList := make([]string, 0, len(colorsSet))
    for word := range colorsSet {
        colorsList = append(colorsList, word)
    }
    sort.Strings(colorsList)
    professionsList := make([]string, 0, len(professionsSet))
    for word := range professionsSet {
        professionsList = append(professionsList, word)
    }
    sort.Strings(professionsList)
    // Print out the 5-word lists for each topic
    fmt.Printf("Foods: %v\n", foodsList[:5])
    fmt.Printf("Cities: %v\n", citiesList[:5])
    fmt.Printf("Books: %v\n", booksList[:5])
    fmt.Printf("Colors: %v\n", colorsList[:5])
    fmt.Printf("Professions: %v\n", professionsList[:5])
}
```
In this implementation, we use a map with string keys and boolean values to implement the hash sets. We loop through each topic and add unique words to the corresponding hash set. Then, we convert each hash set to a sorted array and print out the first 5 words of each list.
This implementation is scalable and efficient because it only stores unique words and sorts the lists once at the end. The use of hash sets also allows for efficient lookup and insertion of words.

