To extract the action and object from a sentence in C#, you can use natural language processing (NLP) techniques. One popular NLP library for C# is the Stanford CoreNLP library.
Here's an example code snippet that uses the Stanford CoreNLP library to extract the action and object from a given sentence:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Stanford.NLP.CoreNLP;
namespace SentenceParser
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Create StanfordCoreNLP object
            var pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP("StanfordCoreNLP.properties");
            // Define the input text
            var input = "John interacts with a literary work";
            // Annotate the input text
            var annotation = new Annotation(input);
            pipeline.Annotate(annotation);
            // Get the sentence from the annotation
            var sentence = annotation.Get(typeof(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation))
                .Cast<CoreLabel>()
                .FirstOrDefault();
            // Get the action and object from the sentence
            var action = sentence.Get(typeof(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation))
                .Cast<CoreLabel>()
                .SkipWhile(token => token.Get(typeof(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation)).ToString() != "VB")
                .FirstOrDefault()
                .Value;
            var obj = sentence.Get(typeof(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation))
                .Cast<CoreLabel>()
                .SkipWhile(token => token.Value != "with")
                .Skip(1)
                .FirstOrDefault()
                .Value;
            // Print the action and object
            Console.WriteLine($"Action: {action}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Object: {obj}");
            Console.ReadKey();
        }
    }
}
```
In this code snippet, we first create a `StanfordCoreNLP` object and define the input text. We then annotate the input text using the `Annotate` method.
Next, we get the sentence from the annotation and extract the action and object from the sentence. To extract the action, we get the first token that has a part of speech tag of "VB" (verb base form). To extract the object, we get the token that follows the word "with".
Finally, we print the action and object to the console. In this case, the output would be:
```
Action: interacts
Object: a literary work
```

